The TCP/IP protocol allows computers of different sizes or vendors to communicate between them, using the same or different operating system.
It has different levels: 
-Physical + Data Link Layers: They manage communication medium details
-Aplication layer: It manages the details of a specific application

+Levels and protocols:
To interconnect two or more networks (and create an internetwork or internet) I need a router:
-Hardware and software with a specific goal that allows to connect different types of physical networks.

Transport and application use protocols end-to-end.
The network layer uses a hop-by-hop protocol that is used in end systems and in every router.
Other LAN interconnection devices: hubs and switches. They implement the physical and data link layers.
Hub: It repeats every frame received through its input ports to all other output ports:
-It only implements the physical layer.
-It works as it was a single LAN network.
Switch: It allows connecting different devices to create a LAN.

+Levels and protocols in TCP/IP:
Application, transport, network, link and physical.

Devices on the internet use an IP address to identify themselves. Each interface must have a 32-bit IP address, where each byte is represented in decimal and separated by dots.
Every IP has two parts, the network ID and the host ID, with a variable size.

Class,Leading Bits,Network ID Size,Host ID Size,Address Range,Use Case
A,0,1 byte,3 bytes,0.0.0.0 to 127.255.255.255,Very large networks
B,10,2 bytes,2 bytes,128.0.0.0 to 191.255.255.255,Medium to large networks
C,110,3 bytes,1 byte,192.0.0.0 to 223.255.255.255,Small networks (LANs)
D,1110,-,-,224.0.0.0 to 239.255.255.255,Multicast
E,1111,-,-,240.0.0.0 to 255.255.255.255,Reserved (Experimental)

Private IP: Used inside your local network (LAN). It is free and can be reused by other people in their own homes.
Public IP: Your "address" on the global Internet. It is unique worldwide and is assigned by your Internet Service Provider (ISP).
NAT (Network Address Translation): The process performed by the router to allow multiple devices with private IPs to access the Internet using a single public IP.

Loopback Interface (Localhost):
The Loopback is a virtual network interface used by a computer to communicate with itself.
IP Range: 127.x.x.x (Class A). Usually 127.0.0.1.
Hostname: localhost.
Key Behavior: Packets sent to loopback never leave the machine (they don't touch the physical network).
Why? To avoid unnecessary network traffic and allow testing of network software without a physical connection.
Multicast/Broadcast: These packets are copied to the loopback interface and sent to the network.

IP Address Types (Unicast, Broadcast, Multicast)
Type	Destination	Protocol Support
Unicast	One specific interface.	TCP / UDP
Broadcast	All machines on the network.	UDP Only
Multicast	A specific group of subscribed machines.	UDP Only
Broadcast Addresses: 255.255.255.255 or <Network_ID>.255.
Problem: Causes overhead because all machines must process the packet up to the Transport Layer before discarding it.
Multicast: Solves the broadcast problem. Machines must "subscribe" to a group. If not subscribed, the network interface discards the packet immediately.
224.0.0.1: All multicast-capable hosts.
224.0.0.2: All routers on the network.

DNS is a distributed database that translates human-readable names into IP addresses.
Function: Maps hostnames (www.google.com) to IPs (8.8.8.8).
Organization: Each entity manages its own DNS server.
Useful commands: * dig <domain> (To query DNS records in Linux).
whois <domain> (To find ownership information).

Port Numbers
If the IP address identifies the computer, the Port Number identifies the specific application (process).
Format: 16-bit number (Range: 1 to 65535).
Well-Known Ports (1 – 1023): Reserved for standard services (Servers).
80: HTTP (Web)
21: FTP (Files)
25: SMTP (Email)
53: DNS
Ephemeral Ports: Temporary ports assigned to clients for a single session.
Linux Tip: Check /etc/services to see the list of ports and services on your system.

